    "_placementAttempts": "Rejection-sampling budget per instance. Raised from the old hardcoded 12 because footprint padding rejects far more candidates, and a low cap under-places silently.",
    "placementAttempts": 40,
    "_nearPathsMetres": "Ground detail is scattered only within this many metres of a trail or an open area, rather than out to the hub's edge. A fern is three parts and stands half a metre tall: four hundred of them spread to the treeline spent most of that budget behind trees nobody walks through. The counts came down with the area, and the result is denser cover where it reads and none where it does not. Trees have no band -- a treeline is precisely the thing you see from a distance."
  },
  "_surfaceLayerMetres": "Ground surfaces overlap on purpose -- trail bends, trail junctions, zone patches -- and two faces on the same plane shimmer as the camera moves. These are the offsets that keep every overlapping surface on its own plane. alternateMetres was 6 mm (0.021 studs), which the depth buffer cannot separate at range: consecutive segments of the same trail fought each other across seven studs while check-zfight, tolerant to 0.015 studs, called it clean. 12 mm (0.043 studs) clears it. The trails sink into a two-metre floor slab, so the extra travel stays invisible.",
  "surfaceLayerMetres": {
    "_trailSurface": "trailBaseMetres is the height of a trail's VISIBLE SURFACE above the forest floor, not the centre of its slab; trailDepthMetres hangs the rest of the slab down into the 2m floor so no trail shows a floating edge from a low angle. Trails do not collide -- the player walks on the floor at y=0 -- so every centimetre here is drawn up the player's leg, and it wants to be as small as it can be. It cannot currently go below 0.118: a trail has to clear every zone slab it crosses, the highest being pondBank at 0.108, and the zone tops 0.072/0.084/0.096/0.108 are exactly one 12mm step apart, so a 12mm ladder either lands on one or misses by 6mm -- under the 9.8mm check-zfight requires. Folding the zone slabs into Surfaces.assignLevels alongside the trails is what would let this drop to a few millimetres.",
    "trailBaseMetres": 0.12,
    "trailDepthMetres": 0.5,
    "stepMetres": 0.026,
    "alternateMetres": 0.012,
    "waterLiftMetres": 0.03
  }
